course: dictionary
lesson: 2409
title: Cutlass: a sailor's short sword
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a thing, not just an action or feeling. «At dusk the Rat dresses everybody for the expedition: a belt, a sword, a cutlass, a pair of pistols...» The list is full of weapons and gear, so a cutlass is probably a kind of weapon too.
explain: Look at the words around it. A sword is a weapon, and a cutlass sits beside it in the list. Also, «Six boatloads of Rats, with pistols and cutlasses, will come up the river» gives clues: boats and the river point to sea or river fights, not the garden.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Put "a short sword" in place of cutlass: «a belt, a sword, a short sword, a pair of pistols...» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A cutlass is a short sword.
fact: A cutlass has a slightly curved blade with one sharp edge.
fact: Sailors and pirates used cutlasses in the past.
fact: «Cutlass» is a noun, and its plural is cutlasses.
fact: Real swords are dangerous weapons, not toys.
exercise: «Each animal got a belt... and then a cutlass on the other side to balance it.» What is a cutlass? | A small boat for the river | A soft cushion | *A short sword with a slightly curved blade | A kind of hat worn by sailors
exercise: In «Six boatloads of Rats, with pistols and cutlasses, will come up the river», which word is a clue that cutlasses belong to sea or river fights? | Rats | garden | land | *boatloads
exercise: Which one of these is a kind of weapon? | sandwich-case | flask | belt | *cutlass
test: Pirates in old stories waved a cutlass. Why is a cutlass dangerous? | *Its blade has one sharp edge | It is made of wood and breaks easily | Sailors use it to catch fish | It is too small to hold
test: In «a belt, a sword, a cutlass, a pair of pistols», which word could replace cutlass and still make sense? | bandages | *short sword | sandwich | flask
exam: When you meet an unknown word like cutlass in a story, what is the best first step? | Stop reading the whole book | Skip the sentence and never come back to it | *Read the words around it and swap in a meaning to check | Guess a colour that fits
